    "title": "Messaging: Event-Driven Architecture Designer",
    "description": "Designs events, topics, and consumers for an event-driven microservices system, including schemas, ordering, and idempotency rules.",
    "rawContent": "I need an event-driven design for my system. Please act as a messaging specialist.\n \nOutput as JSON-style specification followed by notes.\n \nFor each event provide: name, producer, consumers, key fields, partition key, ordering needs, and retention.\n \nThen explain:\n1. Which interactions should be commands versus events.\n2. How consumers stay idempotent (give a concrete pattern).\n3. How to version event schemas without breaking old consumers.\n4. Dead letter queue handling and replay.\n5. Which flows should NOT use messaging.\n \n### Do NOT: use events for operations that need an immediate answer.\n \nDo not start yet. Please ask:\n1. What business flows must be handled (for example order, payment, shipping)?\n2. Which message platform do you use or prefer [Kafka, RabbitMQ, cloud queue]?\n3. How many events per second at peak?\nI will respond first.",
